          %h2#etcs__internalizing_the_logic ETCS : Internalizing the logic
          %p
            This post is a continuation of the discussion of “the elementary theory of the category of sets” [ETCS] which we had begun last time,
            %a{:href => "http://topologicalmusings.wordpress.com/2008/09/01/zfc-and-etcs-elementary-theory-of-the-category-of-sets/"} here
            and in the comments which followed. My thanks go to all who commented, for some useful feedback and thought-provoking questions.
          %p Today I’ll describe some of the set-theoretic operations and “internal logic” of ETCS. I have a feeling that some people are going to love this, and some are going to hate it. My main worry is that it will leave some readers bewildered or exasperated, thinking that category theory has an amazing ability to make easy things difficult.
          %ul
            %li
              %strong An aside:
              has anyone out there seen the book
              = succeed "?" do
                %em Mathematics Made Difficult
              %em are
              category-theory-based books and articles out there which cry out for parody!
          %p In an attempt to nip my concerns in the bud, let me remind my readers that there are major differences between the way that standard set theories like ZFC treat membership and the way ETCS treats membership, and that differences at such a fundamental level are bound to propagate throughout the theoretical development, and impart a somewhat different character or feel between the theories. The differences may be summarized as follows:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Membership in ZFC is a
                %em global
                relation between objects of the
                %em same
                type (sets).
            %li
              %p
                Membership in ETCS is a
                %em local
                relation between objects of
                %em different
                types (“generalized” elements or functions, and sets).
          %p
            Part of what we meant by “local” is that an element
            %em per se
            is always considered relative to a particular set to which it belongs; strictly speaking, as per the discussion last time, the same element is never considered as belonging to two different sets. That is, in ETCS, an (ordinary) element of a set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is defined to be a morphism
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo :
                    %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x : 1 \to A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1 \to B
            of a different set
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ∩
                  %mi B
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi x
                  %mo :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "mediummathspace"}
                  %mo ∧
                  %mspace{:width => "mediummathspace"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \cap B = \{ x : (x \in A) \:\wedge\: (x \in B) \}
          %p
            Instead, in ETCS, what we have is a
            %em local
            intersection operation on
            %em subsets
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi B
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \hookrightarrow X, B \hookrightarrow X
            of a set. But even the word “subset” requires care, because of how we are now treating membership. So let’s back up, and lay out some simple but fundamental definitions of terms as we are now using them.
          %p
            Given two monomorphisms
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi j
                    %mo :
                    %mi B
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i : A \to X, j : B \to X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi j
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i \subseteq j
            (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \subseteq B
            if the monos are understood, or
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %msub
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi X
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \subseteq_X B
            if we wish to emphasize this is local to
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k : A \to B
            such that
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                    %mo =
                    %mi j
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i = j k
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi j
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j
            is monic, there can be at most one such morphism
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
            is monic, such
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            must be monic as well. We say
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                  %mo ,
                  %mi j
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i, j
            %em define the same subset
            if this
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            is an isomorphism. So: subsets of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            are defined to be isomorphism classes of monomorphisms into
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                  %mo ,
                  %mi j
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i, j
            into
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            define the same subset if and only if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi j
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i \subseteq j
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi j
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j \subseteq i
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \subseteq_X
            on monomorphisms thus induces a reflexive, transitive, antisymmetric relation,
            = succeed "," do
              %em i.e.
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            Taking some notational liberties, we write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \subseteq X
            to indicate a subset of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            (as isomorphism class of monos). If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo :
                  %mi U
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x : U \to X
            is a generalized element, let us say
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            %em is in
            a subset
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \subseteq X
            if it factors (evidently uniquely) through any representative mono
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i : A \to X
            = succeed "," do
              %em i.e.
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ′
                  %mo :
                  %mi U
                  %mo →
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x' : U \to A
            such that
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo =
                    %mi i
                    %mi x
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x = i x'
            %em intersection
            of two subsets
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \subseteq X
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B \subseteq X
            is defined to be the subset
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ∩
                  %mi B
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \cap B \subseteq X
            defined by the pullback of any two representative monos
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi j
                    %mo :
                    %mi B
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i : A \to X, j : B \to X
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mspace{:width => "mediummathspace"}
                  %mo ∩
                  %mspace{:width => "mediummathspace"}
                  %mi B
                  %msub
                    %mo ≔
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi X
                  %mo :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mspace{:width => "mediummathspace"}
                  %mtext is in
                  %mspace{:width => "mediummathspace"}
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "mediummathspace"}
                  %mo ∧
                  %mspace{:width => "mediummathspace"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mspace{:width => "mediummathspace"}
                  %mtext is in
                  %mspace{:width => "mediummathspace"}
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \:\cap\: B {\coloneqq}_i \{ x \in X : (x \:\text{is in}\: A) \:\wedge\: (x \:\text{is in}\: B) \}.
          %p
            Thus, intersection works essentially the same way as in ZFC, only it’s
            %em local
            to subsets of a given set.
          %p
            While we’re at it, let’s reformulate the power set axiom in this language: it says simply that for each set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            there is a set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P(B)
            and a subset
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi B
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi B
                    %mo ×
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \in_B \subseteq B \times P(B)
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi B
                    %mo ×
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R \subseteq B \times A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi χ
                    %mi R
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \chi_R : A \to P(B)
            whereby, under
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mn 1
                      %mi B
                    %mo ×
                    %msub
                      %mi χ
                      %mi R
                    %mo :
                    %mi B
                    %mo ×
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                    %mo ×
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1_B \times \chi_R : B \times A \to B \times P(B)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mn 1
                    %mi B
                  %mo ×
                  %msub
                    %mi χ
                    %mi R
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R = (1_B \times \chi_R)^{-1} (\in_B).
          %p The equality is an equality between subsets, and the inverse image on the right is defined by a pullback. In categorical set theory notation,
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi b
                  %mo ,
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi B
                  %mo ×
                  %mi A
                  %mo :
                  %mi b
                  %msub
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi B
                  %msub
                    %mi χ
                    %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R = \{ \langle b, a \rangle \in B \times A : b \in_B \chi_R(a) \}.
          %p Hence, there are natural bijections
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:displaystyle => "true"}
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %mi R
                        %mo ⊆
                        %mi B
                        %mo ×
                        %mi A
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                        %mo →
                        %mi P
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi B
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %mi R
                        %mo ⊆
                        %mi B
                        %mo ×
                        %mi A
                      %mrow
                        %mi B
                        %mo →
                        %mi P
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi A
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \displaystyle \frac{R \subseteq B \times A}{A \to P(B)} \qquad \frac{R \subseteq B \times A}{B \to P(A)}
          %p
            between subsets and classifying maps. The subset corresponding to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi : A \to P(B)
            is denoted
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mo [
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo ]
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi B
                  %mo ×
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \left[\phi\right] \subseteq B \times A
            or
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mo [
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo ]
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi A
                    %mo ×
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \left[\phi\right] \subseteq A \times B
            %em extension
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
          %p
            The set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P(1)
            plays a particularly important role; it is called the “subset classifier” because subsets
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \subseteq X
            are in natural bijection with functions
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi χ
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \chi : X \to P(1)
          %p
            In ordinary set theory, the role of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
