                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle \pi_1, \pi_2 \rangle \in \hom(P(1) \times P(1), P(1)) \times \hom(P(1) \times P(1), P(1))
          %p through the composite
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ×
                  %mi hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mo ∼
                  %mi Sub
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ×
                  %mi Sub
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mo ∩
                  %mi Sub
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mo ∼
                  %mi hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hom(X, P(1)) \times \hom(X, P(1)) \stackrel{\sim}{\to} Sub(X) \times Sub(X) \stackrel{\cap}{\to} Sub(X) \stackrel{\sim}{\to} \hom(X, P(1)).
          %p
            Let’s analyze this bit by bit. The subset
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mo [
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ]
                  %mo =
                  %msubsup
                    %mi π
                    %mn 1
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ×
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \left[\pi_1\right] = \pi_{1}^{-1}(t) \subseteq P(1) \times P(1)
            is given by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi t
                  %mo ×
                  %mi id
                  %mo :
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ×
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ×
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t \times id : 1 \times P(1) \to P(1) \times P(1),
          %p
            and the subset
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mo [
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ]
                  %mo =
                  %msubsup
                    %mi π
                    %mn 2
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ×
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \left[\pi_2\right] = \pi_{2}^{-1}(t) \subseteq P(1) \times P(1)
            is given by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi id
                  %mo ×
                  %mi t
                  %mo :
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ×
                  %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ×
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} id \times t : P(1) \times 1 \to P(1) \times P(1).
          %p
            Hence
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mo [
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ]
                  %mo ∩
                  %mrow
                    %mo [
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ]
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ×
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \left[\pi_1\right] \cap \left[\pi_2\right] \subseteq P(1) \times P(1)
            is given by the pullback of the functions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi t
                  %mo ×
                  %mi id
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t \times id
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi id
                    %mo ×
                    %mi t
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} id \times t
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi t
                  %mo ×
                  %mi t
                  %mo :
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ×
                  %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ×
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t \times t : 1 \times 1 \to P(1) \times P(1).
          %p
            The map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∧
                  %mo :
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ×
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \wedge : P(1) \times P(1) \to P(1)
            is thus defined to be the classifying map of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                    %mo ×
                    %mi t
                    %mo :
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ×
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ×
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t \times t : 1 \times 1 \subseteq P(1) \times P(1)
          %p
            To go from the internal meet
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∧
                  %mo :
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ×
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \wedge : P(1) \times P(1) \to P(1)
            back to the external intersection operation, let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi B
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \subseteq X, B \subseteq X
            be two subsets, with classifying maps
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi χ
                      %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi χ
                      %mi B
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \chi_A, \chi_B : X \to P(1)
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∧
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \wedge
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in X
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi χ
                    %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "mediummathspace"}
                  %mo ∧
                  %mspace{:width => "mediummathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mi χ
                    %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi t
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mtext if and only if
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %msub
                    %mi χ
                    %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi χ
                    %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi t
                  %mo ,
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \chi_A(x) \:\wedge\: \chi_B(x) = t \;\text{if and only if}\; \langle \chi_A(x), \chi_B(x) \rangle = \langle t, t \rangle
          %p
            (where the equality signs are interpreted with the help of equalizers). This holds true iff
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            is in the subset
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \subseteq X
            and is in the subset
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B \subseteq X
            = succeed "," do
              %em i.e.
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            is in the subset
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ∩
                    %mi B
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \cap B \subseteq X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi χ
                    %mi A
                  %mo ∧
                  %msub
                    %mi χ
                    %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \chi_A \wedge \chi_B
            is indeed the classifying map of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ∩
                    %mi B
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \cap B \subseteq X
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi χ
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                        %mo ∩
                        %mi B
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi χ
                      %mi A
                    %mo ∧
                    %msub
                      %mi χ
                      %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \chi_{A \cap B} = \chi_A \wedge \chi_B
          %p A by-product of the interplay between the internal and external is that the internal intersection operator
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∧
                  %mo :
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ×
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \wedge : P(1) \times P(1) \to P(1)
          %p
            is the meet operator of an internal
            %a{:href => "http://topologicalmusings.wordpress.com/2008/04/02/toward-stone-duality-posets-and-meets"} meet-semilattice
            structure on
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P(1)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∧
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \wedge
            is the element
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                    %mo :
                    %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t : 1 \to P(1)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P(1)
            carries an internal poset structure: given generalized elements
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi u
                    %mo ,
                    %mi v
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u, v : A \to P(1)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi u
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi v
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mtext if and only if
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi u
                  %mo =
                  %mi u
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi v
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u \leq v \;\text{if and only if}\; u = u \wedge v
          %p
            and this defines a reflexive, symmetric, antisymmetric relation
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mo [
                      %mo ≤
                      %mo ]
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ×
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \left[\leq\right] \subseteq P(1) \times P(1)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mo [
                    %mo ≤
                    %mo ]
                  %msub
                    %mo ≔
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi u
                  %mo ,
                  %mi v
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ×
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mi u
                  %mo =
                  %mi u
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi v
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \left[\leq\right] {\coloneqq}_i \{ \langle u, v \rangle \in P(1) \times P(1) : u = u \wedge v\},
          %p equivalently described as the equalizer
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mo [
                    %mo ≤
                    %mo ]
                  %mo →
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ×
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mo →
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \left[\leq\right] \to P(1) \times P(1) \stackrel{\to}{\to} P(1)
          %p
            of the maps
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 1
                  %mo :
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ×
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_1 : P(1) \times P(1) \to P(1)
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∧
                    %mo :
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ×
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \wedge : P(1) \times P(1) \to P(1)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi u
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi v
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u \leq v
            if and only if
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mo [
                      %mi u
                      %mo ]
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mrow
                      %mo [
                      %mi v
                      %mo ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \left[u\right] \subseteq \left[v\right]
          %h3#internal_implication Internal Implication
          %p
            Here we begin to see some of the amazing power of the interplay between internal and external logical operations. We will prove that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P(1)
            carries an internal Heyting algebra structure (ignoring joins for the time being).
          %p
            Let’s recall the notion of
            %a{:href => "http://topologicalmusings.wordpress.com/2008/04/08/distributivity-topology-and-heyting-algebras"} Heyting algebra
            in ordinary naive set-theoretic terms: it’s a lattice
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            that has a material implication operator “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⇒
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Rightarrow
            ” such that, for all
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo ,
                    %mi z
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x, y, z \in P
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi y
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi z
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mtext if and only if
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi x
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi y
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mi z
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \wedge y \leq z \;\text{if and only if}\; x \leq y \Rightarrow z.
          %p
            Now: by the universal property of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P(1)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mo :
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ×
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Rightarrow : P(1) \times P(1) \to P(1)
            is uniquely determined as the classifying map of its inverse image
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo ⇒
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ×
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\Rightarrow)^{-1}(t) \subseteq P(1) \times P(1)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi u
                  %mo ,
                  %mi v
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ×
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi u
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mi v
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{ \langle u, v \rangle \in P(1) \times P(1) : (u \Rightarrow v) = t\}
          %p
            Given
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %mi u
                    %mo ,
                    %mi v
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ×
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle u, v \rangle : A \to P(1) \times P(1)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi t
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi u
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mi v
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t \leq u \Rightarrow v
          %p
            (because
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi t
                  %mo :
                  %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t : 1 \to P(1)
            is maximal), which in turn is equivalent to
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi t
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi u
                  %mo =
                  %mi u
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi v
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t \wedge u = u \leq v
          %p
            This means we should define
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mo :
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ×
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Rightarrow : P(1) \times P(1) \to P(1)
            to be the classifying map of the subset
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mo [
                    %mo ≤
                    %mo ]
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ×
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \left[\leq\right] \subseteq P(1) \times P(1).
          %p
            %strong Theorem 1.
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P(1)
            admits internal implication.
          %p
            %strong Proof.
            We must check that for any three generalized elements
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi u
                    %mo ,
                    %mi v
                    %mo ,
                    %mi w
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u, v, w : A \to P(1)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi w
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi u
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mi v
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mtext if and only if
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi w
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi u
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi v
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} w \leq u \Rightarrow v \;\text{if and only if}\; w \wedge u \leq v.
          %p
            Passing to the external picture, let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mo [
                    %mi u
                    %mo ]
                  %mo ,
                  %mrow
                    %mo [
                    %mi v
                    %mo ]
                  %mo ,
                  %mrow
                    %mo [
                    %mi w
                    %mo ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \left[u\right], \left[v\right], \left[w\right]
            be the corresponding subsets of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Rightarrow,
            a generalized element
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi e
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e \in A
            is in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mo [
                    %mi u
                    %mo ⇒
                    %mi v
                    %mo ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \left[u \Rightarrow v\right]
            if and only if
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi u
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi e
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi v
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi e
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u(e) \leq v(e)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi e
                  %mo :
                  %mrow
                    %mo [
                    %mi w
                    %mo ]
                  %mo →
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e: \left[w\right] \to A
            that represents the subset
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mo [
                      %mi w
                      %mo ]
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \left[w\right] \subseteq A
          %p
            %strong Lemma 2.
            The composite
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:displaystyle => "true"}
                    %mi u
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi e
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mrow
                      %mo [
                      %mi w
                      %mo ]
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mi e
                    %mi A
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mi u
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \displaystyle u(e) = (\left[w\right] \stackrel{e}{\to} A \stackrel{u}{\to} P(1))
          %p
            is the classifying map of the subset
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mo [
                      %mi w
                      %mo ]
                    %mo ∩
                    %mrow
                      %mo [
                      %mi u
                      %mo ]
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mrow
                      %mo [
                      %mi w
                      %mo ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \left[w\right] \cap \left[u\right] \subseteq \left[w\right]
          %p
            %strong Proof.
            As subsets of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mo [
                      %mi w
                      %mo ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \left[w\right]
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi u
                  %mi e
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi e
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %msup
                    %mi u
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi e
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mrow
                    %mo [
                    %mi u
                    %mo ]
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mrow
                    %mo [
                    %mi w
                    %mo ]
                  %mo ∩
                  %mrow
                    %mo [
                    %mi u
                    %mo ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (u e)^{-1}(t) = e^{-1} u^{-1}(t) = e^{-1}(\left[u\right]) = \left[w\right] \cap \left[u\right]
          %p
            where the last equation holds because both sides are the subsets defined as the pullback of two representative monos
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi e
                    %mo :
                    %mrow
                      %mo [
                      %mi w
                      %mo ]
                    %mo →
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e : \left[w\right] \to A
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                    %mo :
                    %mrow
                      %mo [
                      %mi u
                      %mo ]
                    %mo →
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i : \left[u\right] \to A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo □
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box
          %p
            Continuing the proof of Theorem 1, we see by Lemma 2 that the condition
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi u
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi e
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi v
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi e
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u(e) \leq v(e)
            corresponds externally to the condition
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mo [
                    %mi w
                    %mo ]
                  %mo ∩
                  %mrow
                    %mo [
                    %mi u
                    %mo ]
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mrow
                    %mo [
                    %mi w
                    %mo ]
                  %mo ∩
                  %mrow
                    %mo [
                    %mi v
                    %mo ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \left[w\right] \cap \left[u\right] \subseteq \left[w\right] \cap \left[v\right]
          %p
            and this condition is equivalent to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mo [
                      %mi w
                      %mo ]
                    %mo ∩
                    %mrow
                      %mo [
                      %mi u
                      %mo ]
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mrow
                      %mo [
                      %mi v
                      %mo ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \left[w\right] \cap \left[u\right] \subseteq \left[v\right]
          %p
            Passing back to the internal picture, this is equivalent to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi w
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi u
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi v
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} w \wedge u \leq v
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo □
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box
          %h3#cartesian_closed_structure Cartesian Closed Structure
          %p
            Next we address a
            %a{:href => "http://topologicalmusings.wordpress.com/2008/09/01/zfc-and-etcs-elementary-theory-of-the-category-of-sets/#comment-469"} comment
            made by “James”, that a category satisfying the ETCS axioms is
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://topologicalmusings.wordpress.com/2008/06/29/basic-category-theory-ii"} cartesian closed
          %p
            %strong Proposition 1.
            If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A, B
            are “sets”, then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ×
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P(A \times B)
            represents an exponential
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi B
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi A
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P(B)^A.
          %p
            %strong Proof.
            By the power set axiom, there is a bijection between maps into the power set and relations:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:displaystyle => "true"}
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %mi ϕ
                        %mo :
                        %mi X
                        %mo →
                        %mi P
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi A
                        %mo ×
                        %mi B
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mrow
                        %mi R
                        %mo ⊆
                        %mi X
                        %mo ×
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi A
                        %mo ×
                        %mi B
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \displaystyle \frac{\phi : X \to P(A \times B)}{R \subseteq X \times (A \times B)}
          %p
            which is natural in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:displaystyle => "true"}
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %mi R
                        %mo ⊆
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi X
                        %mo ×
                        %mi A
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ×
                        %mi B
                      %mrow
                        %mi ϕ
                        %mo ′
                        %mo :
                        %mi X
                        %mo ×
                        %mi A
                        %mo →
                        %mi P
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi B
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \displaystyle \frac{R \subseteq (X \times A) \times B}{\phi' : X \times A \to P(B)}.
          %p
            Putting these together, we have a natural isomorphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ×
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≅
                  %mi hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ×
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hom(-, P(A \times B)) \cong \hom(- \times A, P(B))
          %p
            and this representability means precisely that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ×
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P(A \times B)
            plays the role of an exponential
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi B
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P(B)^A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo □
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box
          %p
            %strong Corollary 1.
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≅
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P(A) \cong P(1)^A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo □
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box
          %p
            The
            %a{:href => "http://topologicalmusings.wordpress.com/2008/07/12/basic-category-theory-iii-representability-adjoints-and-the-yoneda-lemma/"} universal element
            of this representation is an evaluation map
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ×
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \times P(A) \to P(1)
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi A
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi A
                    %mo ×
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \in_A \subseteq A \times P(A)
          %p
            Thus,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi B
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi A
                  %mo ≅
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ×
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P(B)^A \cong P(A \times B)
            represents the set of all functions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi : A \to P(B)
            (that is, relations from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            to
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
          %p
            As per
            %a{:href => "http://topologicalmusings.wordpress.com/2008/09/01/zfc-and-etcs-elementary-theory-of-the-category-of-sets/#comment-469"} our
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "http://topologicalmusings.wordpress.com/2008/09/01/zfc-and-etcs-elementary-theory-of-the-category-of-sets/#comment-470"} discussion
            = succeed "," do
              %em i.e.
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
            where each
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi(a) \subseteq B
            is a singleton, in other words which factor as
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:displaystyle => "true"}
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mi σ
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \displaystyle A \to B \stackrel{\sigma}{\to} P(B)
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi σ
                  %mo :
                  %mi B
                  %mo →
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma : B \to P(B)
            is the singleton mapping:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                  %mo ↦
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi c
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi B
                  %mo :
                  %mi b
                  %mo =
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b \mapsto \{ b \} = \{ c \in B: b = c \}.
          %p
            We see from this set-theoretic description that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi σ
                  %mo :
                  %mi B
                  %mo →
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma : B \to P(B)
            classifies the equality relation
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi b
                  %mo ,
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi B
                  %mo ×
                  %mi B
                  %mo :
                  %mi b
                  %mo =
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi B
                  %mo ×
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{ \langle b, c \rangle \in B \times B: b = c \} \subseteq B \times B
          %p
            which we can think of as either the equalizer of the pair of maps
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 2
                  %mo :
                  %mi B
                  %mo ×
                  %mi B
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_1, \pi_2 : B \times B \to B
            or, what is the same, the diagonal map
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi δ
                      %mi B
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %msub
                      %mn 1
                      %mi B
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mn 1
                      %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %mo :
                    %mi B
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                    %mo ×
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta_B = \langle 1_B, 1_B \rangle : B \to B \times B
          %p
            Thus, we put
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi χ
                      %mi δ
                    %mo :
                    %mi B
                    %mo →
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma = \chi_{\delta} : B \to P(B)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            is a monomorphism. As usual, we get this monomorphism as the pullback
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi χ
                    %mi σ
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \chi_{\sigma}^{-1}(t)
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi t
                  %mo :
                  %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t : 1 \to P(1)
            along
            %em its
            classifying map
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi χ
                      %mi σ
                    %mo :
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \chi_{\sigma} : P(B) \to P(1)
          %p
            Now: a right adjoint such as
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (-)^A
            preserves all limits, and in particular pullbacks, so we ought then to have a pullback
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable{:displaystyle => "false", :rowspacing => "0.5ex"}
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mi B
                            %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                              %mi A
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⟶
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mn 1
                            %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                              %mi A
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                            %mstyle{:scriptlevel => "1"}
                              %mrow
                                %msup
                                  %mi σ
                                  %mi A
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                            %mstyle{:scriptlevel => "1"}
                              %mrow
                                %msup
                                  %mi t
                                  %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} A
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi P
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi B
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                              %mi A
                        %mtd
                          %munder
                            %mo ⟶
                            %mrow
                              %msubsup
                                %mi χ
                                %mi σ
                                %mi A
                        %mtd
                          %mi P
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mn 1
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                              %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\begin{matrix}
                  B^{\mathrlap{A}} &amp; \longrightarrow &amp; 1^{\mathrlap{A}} \\
                  \\mathllap{\scriptsize{\sigma^A}}\downarrow &amp; &amp; \downarrow\mathrlap{\scriptsize{t^\A}} \\
                  P(B)^{\mathrlap{A}} &amp; \underset{\chi_\sigma^A}{\longrightarrow} &amp; P(1)^{\mathrlap{A}}
                  \\end{matrix}
          %p
            Of course, we don’t even have
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi B
                    %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B^A
            yet, but this should give us an idea:
            %em define
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi σ
                      %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma^A
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi B
                      %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B^A
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi B
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi A
                  %mo ×
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≅
                  %mi hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi B
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hom(P(B)^A \times A, P(1)) \cong \hom(P(B)^A, P(1)^A)
          %p to the element in the hom-set (on the left) given by the composite
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:displaystyle => "true"}
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi B
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi A
                    %mo ×
                    %mi A
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mi ev
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi χ
                          %mi σ
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \displaystyle P(B)^A \times A \stackrel{ev}{\to} P(B) \stackrel{\chi_\sigma}{\to} P(1).
          %p
            The map on the right of the pullback is defined similarly. That this recipe really gives a construction of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi B
                    %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B^A
            will be left as an exercise for the reader.
          %h3#universal_quantification Universal Quantification
          %p
            As further evidence of the power of the internal-external dialectic, we show how to internalize
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+quantification"} universal quantification
          %p
            As we are dealing here now with
            %em predicate
            logic, let’s begin by defining some terms as to be used in ETCS and topos theory:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                An
                %em ordinary predicate
                of type
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                is a function
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ϕ
                        %mo :
                        %mi A
                        %mo →
                        %mi P
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi : A \to P(1)
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ϕ
                        %mo ′
                        %mo :
                        %mn 1
                        %mo →
                        %mi P
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn 1
                        %msup
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mi A
                        %mo ≅
                        %mi P
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi A
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi' : 1 \to P(1)^A \cong P(A)
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mrow
                          %mo [
                          %mi ϕ
                          %mo ]
                        %mo :
                        %mi S
                        %mo ⊆
                        %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \left[\phi\right]: S \subseteq A
            %li
              %p
                A
                %em generalized predicate
                of type
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                is a function
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ϕ
                        %mo ′
                        %mo :
                        %mi X
                        %mo →
                        %mi P
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi A
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ≅
                        %mi P
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn 1
                        %msup
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi' : X \to P(A) \cong P(1)^A
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ϕ
                        %mo :
                        %mi X
                        %mo ×
                        %mi A
                        %mo →
                        %mi P
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi : X \times A \to P(1)
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mrow
                          %mo [
                          %mi ϕ
                          %mo ]
                        %mo :
                        %mi S
                        %mo ⊆
                        %mi X
                        %mo ×
                        %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \left[\phi\right] : S \subseteq X \times A
          %p
            We are trying to define an operator
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∀
                    %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \forall_A
            which will take a predicate of the form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo ×
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi : X \times A \to P(1)
            [conventionally written ] to a predicate
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∀
                    %mi A
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \forall_A \phi : X \to P(1)
            [conventionally written ]. Externally, this corresponds to a natural operation which takes subsets of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ×
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \times A
            to subsets of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            = succeed "," do
              %em Internally
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∀
                    %mi A
                  %mo :
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≅
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \forall_A : P(A) \cong P(1)^A \to P(1).
          %p
            This function is determined by the subset
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mo ∀
                      %mi A
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\forall_A)^{-1}(t) \subseteq P(1)^A
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi A
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mo ∀
                    %mi A
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo =
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{ \phi \in P(1)^A : \forall_A \phi = t\}.
          %p
            Now, in ordinary logic,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∀
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi A
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \forall_{a \in A} \phi(a)
            is true if and only if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi(a)
            is true for all
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \in A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi : A \to P(1)
            is constantly true over all of
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:displaystyle => "true"}
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mo !
                    %mn 1
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mi t
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \displaystyle \phi = (A \stackrel{!}{\to} 1 \stackrel{t}{\to} P(1)).
          %p
            The expression on the right (global truth over
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi t
                      %mi A
                    %mo :
                    %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t_A : 1 \to P(1)^A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            is monic. Thus we are led to define the desired quantification operator
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∀
                    %mi A
                  %mo :
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \forall_A : P(1)^A \to P(1)
            as the classifying map of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi t
                      %mi A
                    %mo :
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t_A : 1 \subseteq P(1)^A
          %p
            Let’s check how this works externally. Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi : X \to P(1)^A
            be a generalized predicate of type
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∀
                    %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \forall_A
            has just been defined,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∀
                    %mi A
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \forall_A \phi : X \to P(1)
            classifies the subset
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:displaystyle => "true"}
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi X
                    %mo :
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi t
                      %mi A
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \displaystyle \{ x \in X: \phi(x, -) = t_A : A \to P(1)) \} \subseteq X
          %p
            There is an interesting adjoint relationship between universal quantification and substitution (aka “pulling back”). By “substitution”, we mean that given any predicate
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ψ
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \psi : X \to P(1)
            on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ×
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \times A
            (substituting in a dummy variable
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            of type
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %em e.g.
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ψ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∧
                    %mrow
                      %mo [
                      %mi a
                      %mo =
                      %mi a
                      %mo ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \psi(x) \wedge \left[a=a\right]
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi π
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo ×
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi : X \times A \to X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is the natural external operation
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mrow
                    %mo [
                    %mi ψ
                    %mo ]
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ↦
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mrow
                    %mo [
                    %mi ψ
                    %mo ]
                  %mo ×
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi X
                  %mo ×
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\left[\psi\right] \subseteq X) \mapsto (\left[\psi\right]\times A \subseteq X \times A).
          %p
            Then, for any predicate
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo ×
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi : X \times A \to P(1)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mo [
                    %mi ψ
                    %mo ]
                  %mo ×
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mtext if and only if
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mrow
                    %mo [
                    %mi ψ
                    %mo ]
                  %mo ⊆
                  %msub
                    %mo ∀
                    %mi A
                  %mi ϕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \left[\psi\right] \times A \subseteq \phi \;\text{if and only if}\; \left[\psi\right] \subseteq \forall_A \phi
          %p
            so that substitution along
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is left adjoint to universal quantification along
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
          %h3#internal_intersection_operators Internal Intersection Operators
          %p Now we put all of the above together, to define an internal intersection operator
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋂
                  %mo :
                  %mi PPX
                  %mo →
                  %mi PX
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigcap : PPX \to PX
          %p
            which intuitively takes an element
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %mi PPX
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1 \to PPX
            (a family
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            of subsets of
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %mi PX
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1 \to PX
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋂
                    %mi F
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigcap F \subseteq X
          %p Let’s first write out a logical formula which expresses intersection:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋂
                  %mi F
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mtext if and only if
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mo ∀
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi PX
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in \bigcap F \;\text{if and only if}\; \forall_{S \in PX} (S \in F) \Rightarrow (x \in S).
          %p
            We have all the ingredients to deal with the logical formula on the right: we have an implication operator “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⇒
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Rightarrow
            ” as part of the internal Heyting algebra structure on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P(1)
            = succeed "\u201D." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ∀
                      %mi PX
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \forall_{PX}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (S \in F)
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x \in S)
            refer to internal elementhood:
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x \in S)
            means
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi X
                  %mo ×
                  %mi PX
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle x, S \rangle \in X \times PX
            is in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi X
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi X
                    %mo ×
                    %mi PX
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \in_{X} \subseteq X \times PX
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (S \in F)
            means
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi S
                  %mo ,
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi PX
                  %mo ×
                  %mi PPX
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle S, F \rangle \in PX \times PPX
            is in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi PX
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi PX
                    %mo ×
                    %mi PPX
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \in_{PX} \subseteq PX \times PPX
          %p
            There is a slight catch, in that the predicates “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %msub
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi PX
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (S \in_{PX} F)
            ” and “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %msub
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi X
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x \in_X S)
            ” (as generalized predicates over
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi PX
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} PX
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            lives) are taken over different domains. The first is of the form
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mn 1
                    %mo :
                    %mi PPX
                    %mo →
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi PX
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi_1 : PPX \to P(1)^{PX}
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mn 2
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi PX
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi_2 : X \to P(1)^{PX}
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi PPX
                    %mo ×
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} PPX \times X
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:displaystyle => "true"}
                    %msub
                      %mi ψ
                      %mn 1
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi PPX
                    %mo ×
                    %mi X
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi π
                          %mn 1
                    %mi PPX
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi ϕ
                          %mn 1
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi PX
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \displaystyle \psi_1 = (PPX \times X \stackrel{\pi_1}{\to} PPX \stackrel{\phi_1}{\to} P(1)^{PX})
          %p and
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:displaystyle => "true"}
                    %msub
                      %mi ψ
                      %mn 2
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi PPX
                    %mo ×
                    %mi X
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi π
                          %mn 2
                    %mi X
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi ϕ
                          %mn 2
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi PX
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \displaystyle \psi_2 = (PPX \times X \stackrel{\pi_2}{\to} X \stackrel{\phi_2}{\to} P(1)^{PX}).
          %p Putting all this together, we form the composite
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:displaystyle => "true"}
                    %mi PPX
                    %mo ×
                    %mi X
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                        %msub
                          %mi ψ
                          %mn 1
                        %mo ,
                        %msub
                          %mi ψ
                          %mn 2
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi PX
                    %mo ×
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi PX
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \displaystyle PPX \times X \stackrel{\langle \psi_1, \psi_2 \rangle}{\to} P(1)^{PX} \times P(1)^{PX}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:displaystyle => "true"}
                    %mo ≅
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ×
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi PX
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mo ⇒
                        %msup
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mi PX
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi PX
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mo ∀
                          %mi PX
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \displaystyle \cong (P(1) \times P(1))^{PX} \stackrel{(\Rightarrow)^{PX}}{\to} P(1)^{PX} \stackrel{\forall_{PX}}{\to} P(1)
          %p
            This composite directly expresses the definition of the internal predicate
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋂
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x \in \bigcap F)
            given above. By cartesian closure, this map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi PPX
                  %mo ×
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} PPX \times X \to P(1)
            induces the desired internal intersection operator,
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋂
                    %mo :
                    %mi PPX
                    %mo →
                    %mi PX
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigcap : PPX \to PX
          %p
            This construction provides an important bridge to getting the rest of the internal logic of ETCS. Since we can can construct the intersection of arbitrary definable families of subsets, the power sets
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi PX
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} PX
            are internal inf-lattices. But
            %a{:href => "http://topologicalmusings.wordpress.com/2008/04/04/lattices-and-duality"} inf-lattices are sup-lattices
            as well; on this basis we will be able to construct the colimits (
            = succeed "," do
              %em e.g.
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : X \to Y
            can be factored (in an essentially unique way) as an epi or surjection
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \to I
            to the image, followed by a mono or injection
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi I
                    %mo →
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I \to Y
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            through which
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            factors, by taking the intersection of all such subsets. Image factorization leads in turn to the construction of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/existential+quantification"} existential quantification
          %p As remarked above, the internal logic of a topos is generally intuitionistic (the law of excluded middle is not satisfied). But, if we add in the axiom of strong extensionality of ETCS, then we’re back to ordinary classical logic, where the law of excluded middle is satisfied, and where we just have the two truth values “true” and “false”. This means we will be able to reason in ETCS set theory just as we do in ordinary mathematics, taking just a bit of care with how we treat membership. The foregoing discussion gives indication that logical operations in categorical set theory work in ways familiar from naive set theory, and that basic set-theoretic constructions like intersection are well-grounded in ETCS.
